# Classification Engine

A Java/Spring Boot access control system based on the Bell-LaPadula model 

The Two rules that govern it:
- **No read-up** — you can only read documents at or below your clearance
- **No write-down** — you can only write documents at or above your clearance (so someone can't leak sensitive info into a lower-classified place)

The Four levels: `UNCLASSIFIED`, `CONFIDENTIAL`, `SECRET`, `TOP_SECRET`.

## Lay out

```
controller/   - handles HTTP requests, no logic
service/      - business logic (password hashing, tokens, calls the access check)
security/     - JWT stuff, the request filter, the actual Bell-LaPadula check, Spring Security config
repository/   - Spring Data JPA interfaces
model/        - entities + the ClassificationLevel/AccessType enums
DTO/          - request/response objects so passwords never leak in a response
```

The flow for a protected request: client sends a request with a JWT → filter checks the token's valid and figures out who's making the request → controller pulls out the request data → service calls the access decision logic → if allowed, it hits the database.

Auth (who are you) and authorization (what are you allowed to do) are separate on purpose — you can be logged in fine and still get denied by the classification check.

## Decision I Made

- New users cant pick their own clearance level — everyone starts at `UNCLASSIFIED`. Clearance self assign would be a no go for classfication engine based on access control/   decision
- Passwords are hashed with BCrypt, never stored in plain text.
- Controllers never return raw `User` objects, so the password hash can't accidentally leak out over the API.
- No sessions/cookies — it's all JWT, so CSRF protection is turned off since it's not relevant here.
- If the access check hits something unexpected, it denies by default instead of allowing.

## Stack

Java 21, Spring Boot 4, Spring Security, Spring Data JPA, PostgreSQL, JJWT, Lombok, Docker Compose, JUnit 5.

## Running it

Using Docker Desktop.

and

terminal command

docker-compose up --build


That starts Postgres and the app together. API runs on `http://localhost:8081`.

## Endpoints

| Method | Endpoint         | Needs auth? | What it does |

| POST | `/auth/register`   | No |         Creates an account (always UNCLASSIFIED to start) 
| POST | `/auth/login`      | No |         Logs in, returns a JWT 
| GET  |  `/documents/{id}` | Yes |        Reads a document if your clearance allows it 
| POST | `/documents/write` | Yes |        Creates a document if your clearance allows it 

Protected routes need `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.

## Tests

`AccessDecisionServiceTest` covers all six combinations — equal/higher/lower clearance vs. classification, for both read and write.

## Future Updates I would like to do 

- Right now failures (bad password, access denied) just throw a generic exception and return a 500. I Want to swap that for real exception types mapped to proper status codes (401/403/404).

-Ability to raise someones clearance level after regiser